WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.015
Here's a puzzle.

2
00:00:04.015 --> 00:00:12.151
A payments table in DynamoDB, a database that Amazon runs for you, may take 40,000 writes a second.

3
00:00:12.151 --> 00:00:13.301
It uses 6,000.

4
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:22.273
Yet one record, the running total for the busiest shop, gets 1,500 updates a second, and about 500 of them are refused.

5
00:00:22.273 --> 00:00:25.307
How can a table with so much room say no?

6
00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:26.713
Hold that question.

7
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:29.435
Picture a concert hall's box office.

8
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:35.812
Ten windows hand out booked tickets, each for a range of surnames: A to C, D to F, and so on.

9
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:40.093
Each serves a hundred people a minute, so the office serves a thousand.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.876
Tonight a tour company booked 300 seats under one name, and its guests come one by one.

11
00:00:45.876 --> 00:00:49.337
All of them queue at one window, while nine sit quiet.

12
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:52.421
The thousand a minute was never one big pool.

13
00:00:52.421 --> 00:00:54.475
It was ten windows of a hundred.

14
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:59.784
DynamoDB cuts each table into pieces like those windows, called partitions.

15
00:00:59.784 --> 00:01:11.365
It hashes each item's partition key, the part of the key that says whose item it is, into a big random-looking number, and each partition owns one stretch of those numbers.

16
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:18.976
The windows are the partitions, a booking name is a partition key, and the office's total is the table's capacity.

17
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:21.439
Each partition has a ceiling.

18
00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:28.207
It serves at most 1,000 write units and 3,000 read units a second, and holds about 10 GB.

19
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:32.311
A write unit pays for one write a second of up to 1 KB.

20
00:01:32.311 --> 00:01:36.829
So why doesn't DynamoDB just add partitions when one gets busy?

21
00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.765
It does, for three reasons.

22
00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:40.916
Let's take them one at a time.

23
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.743
Reason one is the capacity you ask for.

24
00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:50.067
On a provisioned table, you set the write units yourself, and each partition gets an equal share.

25
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:58.716
DynamoDB's design paper gives the example: 3,200 write units need 4 partitions of 800.

26
00:01:58.716 --> 00:02:03.903
Raise the table to 3,600, and each share becomes 900.

27
00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:05.784
Now raise it to 6,000.

28
00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:08.429
What happens to the four partitions?

29
00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:09.605
Pause and guess.

30
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:16.605
Each would need 1,500, over the ceiling.

31
00:02:16.605 --> 00:02:20.624
So each splits in two: 8 partitions of 750.

32
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:23.492
Now lower it back to 5,000.

33
00:02:23.492 --> 00:02:24.756
Back to four?

34
00:02:24.756 --> 00:02:25.047
No.

35
00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:27.381
You keep 8, at 625 each.

36
00:02:27.381 --> 00:02:30.006
Partitions only ever split.

37
00:02:30.006 --> 00:02:31.659
They never merge.

38
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.669
A cut cake doesn't glue itself back together.

39
00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:39.207
So lowering the capacity gives you smaller shares, not fewer partitions.

40
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:40.870
Reason two is size.

41
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.343
When a partition nears 10 GB, it splits in two.

42
00:02:44.343 --> 00:02:50.623
In DynamoDB's original design, its share of the units was divided between the halves.

43
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:56.269
A table with 1,000 write units and 15 GB had two partitions of 500.

44
00:02:56.269 --> 00:03:02.921
A key range taking 800 writes a second was refused, while the other half sat idle.

45
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.907
The paper calls this throughput dilution, throughput being the work done per second.

46
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.943
Reason three is traffic.

47
00:03:09.943 --> 00:03:14.592
When a partition stays busy, DynamoDB splits it for consumption.

48
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:22.051
It cuts at a point chosen from the keys it has seen used, not the middle, so the busy keys land on both sides.

49
00:03:22.051 --> 00:03:23.304
That takes minutes.

50
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.626
But two patterns can't be helped.

51
00:03:25.626 --> 00:03:28.719
One is a single item that takes all the traffic.

52
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:37.661
The other is a key range written in order, such as one partition key with a time as its sort key, so every new write lands at the same end.

53
00:03:37.661 --> 00:03:41.491
In the box office, a new window can't split one booking name.

54
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.220
And there's our puzzle.

55
00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:47.455
The shop's running total is one item, so it lives in one partition.

56
00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:57.764
1,500 updates a second against a ceiling of 1,000: about 500 a second are refused, however much room the table has.

57
00:03:58.033 --> 00:04:01.980
Refused has a name: DynamoDB throttles the request.

58
00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:09.332
It answers with HTTP 400, the web's code for a request it won't carry out, and changes nothing.

59
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:16.827
Amazon's library for your code, the AWS SDK, retries a few times, waiting longer before each try.

60
00:04:16.827 --> 00:04:20.661
So first you see slower answers, then an exception.

61
00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:24.769
The exception carries throttling reasons, which name the limit.

62
00:04:24.769 --> 00:04:28.768
Table write key range throughput exceeded means a hot partition.

63
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:34.016
Table write provisioned throughput exceeded means the whole table used up its units.

64
00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:36.008
More capacity fixes the second.

65
00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:37.821
It does nothing for the first.

66
00:04:37.821 --> 00:04:42.175
Adding windows to the box office doesn't shorten the tour group's queue.

67
00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:45.531
So read the reason before you raise the capacity.

68
00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:46.995
One more trap.

69
00:04:46.995 --> 00:04:52.235
DynamoDB Local is a single Java program for testing on your laptop.

70
00:04:52.235 --> 00:04:55.520
It has no partitions and ignores capacity.

71
00:04:55.700 --> 00:05:01.554
In the lesson's lab, a table of 1 write unit gets 1,000 writes from eight threads.

72
00:05:01.554 --> 00:05:03.196
How many are throttled?

73
00:05:03.196 --> 00:05:04.339
Pause and guess.

74
00:05:07.567 --> 00:05:07.928
Zero.

75
00:05:07.928 --> 00:05:09.448
Local can't throttle.

76
00:05:09.448 --> 00:05:15.309
So to test how your code handles a throttle, use the lab's model or a real table.

77
00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:23.616
So the moral of the story: a table's capacity is a sum of partition ceilings, and one record lives behind one of them.

78
00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:24.927
One quick check.

79
00:05:24.927 --> 00:05:29.167
Your exception says table write provisioned throughput exceeded.

80
00:05:29.167 --> 00:05:31.751
Will raising the table's capacity help?

81
00:05:31.751 --> 00:05:32.811
Pause and think.

82
00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:36.371
Yes.

83
00:05:36.371 --> 00:05:43.056
That reason is the table's own budget, not one hot partition, so more units are the fix.

84
00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:50.188
Next, we'll see how DynamoDB lends a busy partition the table's spare units, and where that help stops.
